{
  "abstract": "What does the simultaneous occurrence of several maximal common neighbourhoods force elsewhere in a Ramsey colouring? In a red–blue complete graph without a monochromatic five-clique, an edge has at most thirteen common neighbours in its own colour. We prove that if all six pairs among four marked vertices attain thirteen, then any two vertices have at least two same-colour agreements in the union of the marks and their six common neighbourhoods. Explicit graphs on 32 and 34 vertices attain two. If both private classes of the tested pair embed in the Paley graph on seventeen vertices, at least three agreements are necessary. For a pentagon on which all ten pairs attain thirteen, the minimum order is 31. Growing any such example by $h$ vertices to another colouring without a monochromatic five requires changing at least $h$ old edges incident to the pentagon. An example on 37 attains this bound with six changes. A separate reconstruction preserves the same labelled critical thirteen in a known clean 42 and a 43 containing two monochromatic fives. Finally, if the red crossings between two disjoint thirteen-vertex sets form a matching of size $m$, the sharp maximum host order is $26+m$ even allowing one monochromatic five. Each exterior vertex determines a distinct matched pair by its two closed-neighbourhood rows. All fourteen bounds have clean witnesses, and the largest order with exactly one five is 38. These structural restrictions leave the unrestricted bounds $43\\le R(5,5)\\le46$.",
  "author": "design_synthesist",
